@startuml join-handshake
!theme plain
title Join handshake (revision 3): a server challenge, a signed canonical transcript, atomic invitation consumption; anchors: the Web PKI, DNSSEC validated in the client (default), and the invitation's pins (fallback for an environment without a DNSSEC domain)

participant "Donor bridge identity\n(curator remote-worker init)" as C
database "DNS: system resolver,\nDNS-over-HTTPS as the fallback\n(signatures validated inside the client)" as R
participant "Join point\n(our host)" as JP
actor "Operator" as OP
participant "Registry / key keeper" as T

note over C, T
  Anchors before first contact:
  (a) the Web PKI certificate of the join point's domain;
  (b) default: DNSSEC-secure TXT _swarma.<domain> = root fingerprint, TXT _swarma-ssh-ca.<domain> = our SSH host CA key, TLSA = the join point's TLS key, validated inside the client from the embedded root trust anchor (a closed network brings its own zone trust anchor);
  (c) the invitation (marketplace deal thread, e-mail, our site) with the same fingerprints and a one-use random code (>= 128 bits): the fallback when the environment has no DNSSEC domain, a cross-check otherwise.
  Proof of possession: the transcript signed over a SERVER challenge (not a client nonce).
  No human second channel and nothing for the donor to configure. No step downgrades on failure.
end note

C -> JP: POST /join/challenge {invitation_id}
JP -> JP: invitation known, unexpired, not consumed? attempt counter
JP --> C: server_challenge (5 min)
C -> R: TXT _swarma, TXT _swarma-ssh-ca, TLSA
R --> C: records + validation state (secure | bogus | indeterminate)
C -> C: secure: must equal the invitation when present; bogus: refuse;\nindeterminate: retry over DNS-over-HTTPS, then refuse (dns_unverifiable);\nan invitation that declares no DNSSEC domain: proceed on its pins, recorded
C -> C: transcript = canonical CBOR, domain-separated:\n{version, invitation_id, project, role, K_sig, K_enc, K_ssh, server_challenge, client_nonce, expires_at, algorithms}
C -> JP: HTTPS: Web PKI for the domain, pinned by the TLSA record (and the invitation when present)
C -> JP: POST /join/cert {transcript, sig_K_sig(transcript), code}
JP -> JP: code equal (constant time)? signature valid? challenge bound and fresh?\nconsume the invitation atomically -> tombstone(invitation_id, K_sig)\n(same K_sig retry -> same pending enrolment; other key -> refuse); rate limit
JP -> OP: show {invitation_id, fingerprint, name, role}
OP -> OP: check against the donor's signed marketplace card when there is one,\notherwise the invitation the code binds; no voice check
OP -> T: pin K_sig, K_enc, K_ssh; root-signed admission; grants (scope, expiry)
OP -> OP: package: AEAD to K_enc with AAD = H(transcript); signed by the operator certificate (root-signed, scope onboard)
OP -> JP: release package for <invitation_id>
C -> JP: POST /join/challenge {invitation_id} (second challenge)
C -> JP: POST /join/collect {invitation_id, sig_K_sig(second challenge)}
JP --> C: package
C -> C: decrypt with K_enc; verify operator cert chain to the pinned root;\nverify package signature; check AAD; pin our SSH host CA key from the package\nAND cross-check it against DNS (or the invitation)

note over C, T
  Pinned afterwards:
  on our side K_sig, K_enc, K_ssh in the registry; K_ssh reaches the workplace user only through short certificates of our user CA (TrustedUserCAKeys);
  on the donor side our root, the operator certificate, our SSH host CA key (@cert-authority), the binding digest, the qualified tuple, the policy generation.
  Every later message: signed over exact bytes by K_sig on the donor, fresh, replay-checked, grant-checked (ADM).
  Replay: cert/collect meet the tombstone; frames meet connection-scoped ids and op_key caches.
  Rotation: a new host key gets a new host certificate (no DNS change); a host CA rotation publishes the new key beside the old; operator cert -> root-signed record; K -> revoke + new enrolment.
end note
@enduml
